<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; border: 1px solid #a7d9ed;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin-bottom: 10px;">Lesson Planner – Theme: Learning to Relax – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Learning to Balance</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Learning to Relax</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Comprehend complex narrative texts. Reading: Analyze persuasive pieces on current events.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can comprehend narratives about balancing school, social activities, and personal life (e.g., "The speaker described how they balance their academic work with social activities like volunteering. They explained how managing time well helps them stay organized and reduce stress.")
Can analyze persuasive pieces about managing relaxation effectively (e.g., "The article argues that students who set clear boundaries between school and personal time tend to have lower stress levels.")
Can discuss the benefits of balancing school work and relaxation (e.g., "The story suggests that having time for both schoolwork and relaxation helps students stay focused.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #e0f2f7; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 60%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 10%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images depicting students studying intensely, exercising, spending time with friends, and practicing mindfulness. Students are asked: "What feelings do these images evoke? How do they relate to the challenge of balancing school activities, social life, and personal wellbeing?" Students briefly share their initial thoughts, activating prior knowledge and vocabulary related to *stress*, *wellbeing*, and *time management* from the previous lesson. The teacher asks, "What have you been doing to balance your daily routine lately?" prompting use of the present perfect continuous.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models reading a short paragraph about the importance of breaks from a similar text, demonstrating fluent reading and emphasizing key vocabulary. For example, "Many students *have been struggling* to maintain a healthy *balance* between their *workload* and *relaxation*. It's crucial to *prioritize* personal *wellbeing*." The teacher also models the pronunciation distinction between /ɪ/ in "*activities*" and /iː/ in "*relieve*," repeating sentences like, "Balancing school *activities* can help *relieve* stress."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key terms like "procrastination" and "mindfulness."</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you *procrastinate*, do you finish tasks early or delay them until the last minute?" (Expected answer: Delay them.)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is *mindfulness* about constantly worrying, or about focusing on the present moment and being aware of your feelings?" (Expected answer: Focusing on the present moment.)</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Learning to Balance".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive an online article titled "The Art of Sustainable Student Life: Finding Your Balance" from a youth-oriented lifestyle blog. (Genre: persuasive article with narrative elements, Register: semi-formal/informative, Format: digital text with subheadings and short paragraphs). The article incorporates vocabulary such as *balance, routine, workload, boundaries, flexibility, stress, relaxation, mindfulness, wellbeing, prioritize, allocate, procrastinate*, and grammatical structures like the present perfect continuous, second conditional, and modals for possibility.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Comprehend complex narrative texts. Reading: Analyze persuasive pieces on current events.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> Students read the title and first paragraph. "What is the main purpose of this article? What problem is it trying to address for students?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements:</b> Students read the entire article quickly to determine if 4-5 statements are true or false based on the main arguments. For example: "The article suggests that students should avoid social activities to improve their academic performance." (False)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match each subheading in the article (e.g., "Setting Clear Boundaries," "The Power of Downtime," "Embracing Flexibility") to a short summary sentence provided by the teacher.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading strategies for specific information, identifying grammatical structures (present perfect continuous, second conditional, modals for possibility) and target vocabulary in context. Students will practice reading for detailed understanding and textual analysis.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Vocabulary in Context (Controlled Practice):</b> Students re-read the article more carefully. They underline all the target vocabulary words (e.g., *routine, workload, prioritize, mindfulness, boundaries, allocate, procrastination*) and then, individually, write a short definition or synonym for each word based on its usage in the text. The teacher circulates, providing support and clarifying meanings.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Grammar Spotting & Analysis (Semi-controlled):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher gives them specific sentence beginnings or grammar patterns to look for. For example, "Find two sentences where the author discusses ongoing efforts using the present perfect continuous (e.g., 'Students *have been trying*...')." "Identify a sentence that expresses a hypothetical situation for improving balance using the second conditional (e.g., 'If I *had* more free time, I *would*...')." Students then discuss how these structures contribute to the author's message.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Reading for Suggestions (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> In pairs, students identify sections of the article where the author gives suggestions for balancing social life, schoolwork, and relaxation. They specifically look for sentences using modals for possibility or advice (e.g., "You *might need* to set aside time...", "Students *should* practice mindfulness..."). They highlight these sentences and discuss what suggestions are being made, checking each other's interpretations against the text.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Learning to Balance".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Group Analysis and Discussion.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> Students will apply their reading comprehension skills to analyze the persuasive elements of the article and discuss its relevance to their own lives, connecting directly to "Learning to Balance" and "Learning to Relax."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity:</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group is assigned one major section or argument from the article. Their task is to:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Read their assigned section deeply to identify the main problem addressed and the primary solution(s) proposed by the author for balancing life.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Discuss within their group: Do they agree with the author's arguments? Why or why not? They must use target vocabulary (e.g., *boundaries, workload, effective, healthy balance*) and grammatical structures from the lesson (e.g., "If students *prioritized* downtime, they *would experience* less *stress*." "I think the article suggests we *should* integrate *mindfulness* into our *routine*.") to express their opinions and give suggestions.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Prepare to share their group's main takeaways, including their agreement/disagreement and any alternative strategies, in a brief whole-class discussion. This encourages inclusive communication where all group members share their cultural perspectives and contribute strategies for managing stress effectively.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group discussion and sharing, focusing on comprehension and application of target language.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the group analysis and the subsequent whole-class discussion, demonstrating their comprehension of the article and ability to articulate their opinions and suggestions based on its content.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to identify main ideas, arguments, and supporting details from the reading text.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., *balance, routine, stress, mindfulness, prioritize, boundaries*).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of grammar (present perfect continuous, second conditional, modals for possibility) to analyze arguments and give suggestions.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engagement in collaborative discussion, encouraging inclusive communication.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and intelligibility of contributions (teacher observes pronunciation, especially of /ɪ/ and /iː/ sounds in context).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of pragmatic functions: expressing opinions on balance and giving suggestions.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. "What was the most important strategy for balancing school and relaxation that you learned about from the article today?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. "Which new vocabulary word or grammatical structure from the article did you understand better today, and how will you try to use it?"</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. "How well do I feel I comprehended the main arguments and specific details of the article about balancing school, social life, and relaxation?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. "What reading strategy (e.g., skimming for gist, scanning for details, analyzing grammar) did I find most helpful today, and why?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully comprehend both the narrative elements and persuasive arguments of the B1.3 level text? Were the texts and tasks appropriately challenging for 12th graders?</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the practice activities effective in helping students identify and understand the target grammar (present perfect continuous, second conditional, modals) and vocabulary within the reading context?</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolding might be needed for students who struggled with analysis or expressing opinions in the group task? For instance, more sentence starters for giving suggestions or expressing hypothetical situations.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "In the next lesson, we will transition from reading about balance to actively discussing and sharing our own strategies and experiences for managing school, social life, and relaxation. We will focus on speaking and interaction, using the vocabulary and grammatical structures we practiced today to express our ideas and listen to our peers' perspectives."</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>